[Theorem 7 of arXiv:2607.16145, 'The Memoryless Best-Choice Problem'] An  optimal threshold  $f^*$ in the ML best-choice problem satisfies the 
equation
\begin{equation}
e^{-f^*(t)}= J(f^*;t), ~~t\in [0,1),
\end{equation}
which implies the key identity ${\mathbb E}\tau^*=v$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition prop:poly-approx of arXiv:2512.04371, 'Constructive Approximation under Carleman's Condition, \\ with Applications to Smoothed An'] Suppose that $\varepsilon,\sigma>0$. 
Let $\mu$ be a probability measure on $\RR^d$. 
Then, for any functions $f\in \cH(k): \RR^d\to \{\pm 1\}$. 

there exists a polynomial $P$ with total degree 
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $O\Big(\big(  \frac{{\log (\varepsilon^{-1})  + k\log(1/\sigma)}} {\sigma^2}\big)^{r/2}\Big)$ under $r$-strictly sub-exponential $\mu$ with $r\le 2$;
  \item  $O\Big(\big( \frac {\log (\varepsilon^{-1})  + k\log(k/\sigma)}{\sigma^2}\big)^{r/2}\Big)$ under $r$-strictly sub-exponential $\mu$ with $r>2$; 
  \item $O\big(  \big(\varepsilon^{-1} (k/\sigma)^k\big)^{O(1/\sigma)}\big)$ under sub-exponential $\mu$,
\end{enumerate}

such that $\norm{T_\sigma f - P}_{L^2(\mu)} \le \varepsilon$.

[Lemma 125 (Lemma 6.5  \cite{Kr_26_1}) of arXiv:2605.03236, 'Stochastic It\^o Equations and Parabolic Second-Order Equations with singular Drift'] Let $p,q\in(1,\infty)$,  $g(t,x)\geq 0$ be a Borel function such that for any smooth bounded $f(t,x)$ we have
(bounded linear functional  of special type)
\begin{equation}

\int_{\bR^{d+1}}g|f|\,dxdt\leq
\|f\|_{E_{q,p,\beta}}. 
\end{equation}
Then, for any $f\in E_{q,p,\beta}$,
\eqref{2.16.4} holds and, moreover,
$$
\lim_{\varepsilon\downarrow 0}
\int_{\bR^{d+1}}
g|f-f^{(\varepsilon)}|\,dxdt=0.
$$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Thm oracle_split of arXiv:2604.07183, 'Stopping on the last success with unknown odds: asymptotic minimax optimality of the plug-'] Fix~$p_0\in(0,1)$ and~$a\in(0,1)$, and let~$M_0$, $\mathcal B_{p_0}$ and~$\Delta_p$ be as in Theorem~(thm:finite_oracle_ineq_p0 of the paper). Then, (i) there exist positive constants~$C_1(p_0)$, $C_2(p_0,a)$ and~$c(p_0,a)$ such that, for all~$n\ge2$ and all~$p\in[p_0,1)$,
\begin{equation}

V_n(p)-W_n^{\pi_a}(p)
\le
C_1(p_0) \Delta_p\,e^{-2\lfloor an\rfloor\Delta_p^2}
+
C_2(p_0,a)e^{-c(p_0,a)n}
.
\end{equation}
(ii) Consequently, for~$p_0\le\frac12$, there exists a positive constant~$C(p_0)$ such that
\begin{equation}

\sup_{p\in[p_0,1)}\bigl(V_n(p)-W_n^{\pi_a}(p)\bigr)
\le
\frac{C(p_0)}{\sqrt{an}}
\end{equation}
for all~$n$ large enough. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition prop:CLT for V of arXiv:2606.28031, 'Insurance risk models in a heterogeneous time-dependent population: scaling limits and rui'] Under Assumptions~(A:hyp LLN for V of the paper) and~(A:hyp CLT for V of the paper), if $X(1)$ and $Y(1)$ have finite second moments, then
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{t}}\PAR{V(t)-\mathbb{E}[V(t)]}\overset{\rm d}{\underset{t\to \infty}{\longrightarrow}}\mathscr{Z}^{\rm c}   .
\end{equation}

