{"artifact_digest":"sha256:2f4c30e77ab28dc8cae450437538332db7d19df9672b4f95041c4a178a673920","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/2f/2f4c30e77ab28dc8cae450437538332db7d19df9672b4f95041c4a178a673920.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:f6b5bb5b13592a1a47d5c691a84a4703caff48b0458aa66755e6bd80f9fe8c0b","bundle_entry_key":"2604-25851-theorem-9-he-infin-many","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_105","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_105.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2604-25851-proposition-9-he-infin-many","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: It is immediate that $u^z$ also solves the interpolated FPE (equation eq:HE-beta of the paper), since it satisfies both the pure-drift equation (equation eq:HE-divergenceform of the paper) (as argued there) and the pure-diffusion equation (the heat equation). 
We want to apply Theorem (thm:Markov-construction of the paper) together with Lemma (lem:extrem-unique-equiv of the paper).
    Let $z \in \mathbb{R}^d$ and $s>0$. We need to show that the linear FPE
    \begin{equation}
