{"artifact_digest":"sha256:6e182a3a2c228e3be86410ebbb9ecfcce2061fb508a7577723d5ffeaf43af8a9","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/6e/6e182a3a2c228e3be86410ebbb9ecfcce2061fb508a7577723d5ffeaf43af8a9.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:6c7981e2f5ca8fe6fa582c13f72b852b8a0f6b06f72f0b955f4b677c8f9ef561","bundle_entry_key":"2506-10476-lemma-17-subset-inclusion","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s2_002","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s2_002.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2506-10476-lemma-17-subset-inclusion","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: To do so, we will be using the Shape Theorem for standard \textsc{idla}, in the case where exactly $n$ particles are sent from the origin. Let us denote this aggregate by $A(n)$. We know from Theorem 1 of \cite{lawler1992internal} that 
