body:
[Definition 11 (Free Brownian motion) of arXiv:2606.31528, 'Well-posedness and stationary distribution of free stochastic differential equations'] A stochastic process $\{W_t\}_{t\ge 0}$ is called a \emph{free Brownian motion} if it satisfies 
\begin{itemize}
\item $W_0=0;$
\item The increment $W_t-W_s$ is freely independent from $W^*(W_r: 0\leq r\leq s)$ for all $0\leq s <t;$
\item The increment $W_t-W_s$ is a semicircular element with mean $0$ and variance $t-s$ for all $0 \leq s <t.$
\end{itemize}

