[Proposition 8 of arXiv:2607.01629, 'Quadratic fluctuations of speed-change Kawasaki dynamics'] For every test function $f$, the sequence of martingales and the corresponding quadratic variation  
        \[
        \left\{\mathcal M^N_t(f), t\in [0,T]\right\}_{N \in \N}, \quad \left\{\la\mathcal M^N(f)\ra_t, t\in [0,T]\right\}_{N \in \N}
        \]
         are tight in $D([0, T], \mathbb{R})$ and and all limit points are concentrated on $C\left([0,T],\R\right)$. If we have the following weak convergence in $D\left([0,T], \S' \left(\T^{2d}\right)\right)$ along a subsequence $N_k\to\infty$
         \begin{align}
			(\M^{N_k}_t)_{t \in [0,T]} \xRightarrow{N_k \to \infty} (\M_t)_{t \in [0,T]},
		\end{align}
         then the limit process $\mathcal M_t$ is a continuous martingale and the following weak joint convergence holds 
         \begin{equation}
         \Ll(\mathcal M^{N_k}_t,\la\mathcal M^{N_k}\ra_t\Rr)_{t\in [0,T]}  \xRightarrow{N_k \to \infty} \Ll(\M_t,\la\mathcal M\ra_t\Rr)_{t \in [0,T]}.
         \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

