body:
[Theorem 5 of arXiv:2608.13235, 'Mixing Properties of Random Laguerre Tessellations'] Let $\ppp$ be a stationary marked point process on $\rd \times \R$ with a stationary mark distribution $\markdist$. Assume that there exists $\delta > 0$ such that the typical mark $M \sim \markdist$ satisfies 
\begin{equation} 
\e M_-^\frac{d+\delta}{2}< \infty,
\end{equation} 
where $M_-$ is the negative part of $M$. Then the following implications hold.
\begin{itemize}
    \item[(a)] If $\ppp$ is ergodic, then $L(\ppp)$ is \as a tessellation of $\rd$ and it is also ergodic.
    \item[(b)] If $\ppp$ is mixing, then $L(\ppp)$ is mixing.
    \item[(c)] If $\ppp$ is $\alpha$-mixing, then $L(\ppp)$ is $\alpha$-mixing.
\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Prop quantunique of arXiv:2507.09120, 'Chemical distance in graphs of polynomial growth'] Let $p_0 > p_c$. Then there exists $R_0 < \infty$ such that for all $R \ge R_0$, we have
   \[
      \mathbb{P}_p( A_v) \ge 1 - e^{- \sqrt{R}}
   \]
   for all $p \in [p_0,1]$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lem:scaling of arXiv:2608.05089, 'Correlation decay in area-tilted line ensembles'] For any $\rho > 0$, we have
\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{X} \sim \mathcal{L}^{\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},\bm{\lambda}}_{n,\ell,r,\mathbf{f},\mathbf{g}}
    \qquad \text{if and only if} \qquad
    \mathbf{X}^{(\rho)} \sim \mathcal{L}^{\rho \mathbf{x}, \rho \mathbf{y}, \rho^{-3} \bm{\lambda}^{(\rho)}}_{n, \rho^2 \ell, \rho^2 r, \mathbf{f}^{(\rho)}, \mathbf{g}^{(\rho)}}.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

