[Thm star<->complete of arXiv:2606.29829, ''] For every $n\geq 1$, let $\mathfrak{f}_n:S_n\rightarrow \{0,1\}$ be a Boolean function. Then,
  	\begin{enumerate}
  		\item The sequence $\{\mathfrak{f}_{n}\}_{n=1}^{\infty}$ is noise sensitive with respect to the interchange process on $K_{n}$ implies that it is noise sensitive with respect to the interchange process on $\ST$.
  		\item The sequence $\{\mathfrak{f}_{n}\}_{n=1}^{\infty}$ is noise stable with respect to the interchange process on $\ST$ implies that it is noise stable with respect to the interchange process on $K_{n}$.
  	\end{enumerate}
  	The \emph{dictator function} given in Example (ex:dictator of the paper) ensures that the converses are not true. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition def:unimodularity of arXiv:2505.07145, 'Nonamenable Poisson zoo'] Let $\Gamma$ be a transitive subgroup of automorphism of the graph $G$. If we have the \textbf{mass transport principle (MTP)}
	\begin{equation}

		\sum_{y \in V(G)} f(y,x) =
		\sum_{y \in V(G)} f(x,y)
	\end{equation}
	for any diagonally $\Gamma$-invariant $f  :  V(G) \times V(G) \longrightarrow [0, \infty]$ function and for any $x \in V(G)$, then we say that the action of $\Gamma$ is \textbf{unimodular}.	If the action of $\mathrm{Aut}(G)$ is unimodular on $G$, then we simply say that $G$ is unimodular.

