{"artifact_digest":"sha256:652cfbc0bef69d1854e0ab2630eaece87024064841e357478219eed0aecb1fdf","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/65/652cfbc0bef69d1854e0ab2630eaece87024064841e357478219eed0aecb1fdf.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:98d69e54c7b3c3970f0b448b1de200ace784abdff8eab18a6462c1cc97c00154","bundle_entry_key":"2608-30967-theorem-5-riesz","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_131","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_131.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2608-30967-thm-5-riesz","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: (i) Suppose $\{x_k\}_{k \in \mathcal{K}}$  is a b-frame for $\mathcal{Z}$. To prove the equivalence of Statements \emph{(1)} and \emph{(2)}, we just need to prove that the operator $U$ in Lemma \ref{RieszL1} is one-to-one if and only if $\{x_k\}_{k \in \mathcal{K}}$  is a b-basis for $\mathcal{Z}$.

Let $z \in \mathcal{Z}$. Since $\{E_k\}_{k \in \mathcal{K}}$ is a b-orthonormal basis for $\mathcal{Z}$, $z$ has a unique representation in the form
\begin{eqnarray}
z= \sum_{k \in \mathcal{K}}b(h_k,E_k).
\end{eqnarray}
Therefore,
\begin{eqnarray}
