[Lemma homotopy-po-equiv-fixed-points of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] A commutative square 
    \begin{equation}
        \begin{tikzcd}
            A\arrow[r]\arrow[d] & B\arrow[d]\\
            C\arrow[r] & D
        \end{tikzcd}
    \end{equation}
    in $\textbf{\textup{$\bm G$-Top}}$ is a homotopy pushout in the ${\mathcal{F}}$-model structure if and only if for every $H\in{\mathcal{F}}$ the resulting square on $H$-fixed points is a homotopy pushout in $\textbf{\textup{Top}}$.
    \begin{proof}
        Pick a factorization of $A\to B$ into a cofibration $i$ followed by an ${\mathcal{F}}$-weak equivalence $f$ as depicted in the following diagram:
        \[
            \begin{tikzcd}
                A\arrow[r,"i"]\arrow[d]\arrow[dr,phantom,"\ulcorner"{very near end}] & B_0\arrow[r,"f","\sim"']\arrow[d] & B\arrow[d]\\
                C\arrow[r] & C_0\arrow[r,dashed,"g"'] & D\rlap.
            \end{tikzcd}
        \]
        By definition, $((diag:orig-square of the paper))$ is a homotopy pushout if and only if $g$ is an ${\mathcal{F}}$-weak equivalence. As $i$ is in particular an h-cofibration, so is $i^H$ for every $H\in{\mathcal{F}}$ by Remark~(rk:fixed-and-restr-preserve-h-cof of the paper); moreover, [schwede2018global]*{Proposition~B.1(i)} shows that if we apply $H$-fixed points to the left-hand square in the above diagram, this is still an (honest) pushout square. Thus, applying the previous lemma for $G=1$, we see that $g^H$ is a weak homotopy equivalence if and only if $((diag:orig-square of the paper))$ induces a homotopy pushout on $H$-fixed points. Letting $H$ vary, this yields precisely the claim.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lem bridge of arXiv:2508.04954, 'Conditional exponential directed last passage percolation under a one-point upper large de'] Let $m\ge 2$. 
Let $\Gamma_1, \cdots, \Gamma_m$ be disjoint contours, listed from left to right, each parallel to the $y$-axis with upwards orientation. 
Let $\vec\Gamma \equiv \Gamma_1\times\cdots\times\Gamma_m$. 

For every $0=a_0<a_1<\cdots<a_m = A$ and $b_1,\cdots,b_{m-1}\in{\mathbb R}$ with $b_0=b_m=0$,  
\begin{equation*}
    \frac{\sqrt{2\pi A}}{(2\pi\mathrm{i})^m} \int_{\vec\Gamma} 
    \frac{\prod_{i=1}^m e^{\frac{1}{2}(a_i-a_{i-1})u_i^2+(b_{i}-b_{i-1})u_i} }{\prod_{i=1}^{m-1}(u_{i+1}-u_i)}  \mathrm{d} {\mathbf u}    
    = \mathbb{P}\left({\sqrt{A} \, \mathbb{B}^{\mathrm{br}}\left(\frac{{a_{i}}}{A}\right)>b_i, \,\,  i=1,\dots,m-1}\right)
\end{equation*}
where $\mathrm{d}{\mathbf u}= \mathrm{d} u_1\cdots\mathrm{d} u_m$  with each $u_i\in\Gamma_i$, and $\mathbb{B}^{\mathrm{br}}$ is a standard Brownian bridge.

[Lem cor:ellcov of arXiv:2602.22342, 'Sum of Gaussian vectors and large sets'] Given a closed symmetric set  $S\subset \R^n$ and  $\tau>0$, the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Any ellipsoid $E$ with trace $\Tr E=\tau$ intersects $S$.
\item There is a random vector $X$ supported on $S$ such that $$X\sim -X\quad\text{and}\quad \|\Cov X\|\leq \tau^{-1} .$$
\end{enumerate}

