{"artifact_digest":"sha256:f8b8fc93883ba6e93b9365048638c94a01c04a423496018d3ef6867028f615e2","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/f8/f8b8fc93883ba6e93b9365048638c94a01c04a423496018d3ef6867028f615e2.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:89bdbbba75cb70c89e709e37f8e6212ef9336dbbbc6702f4aaf510c1a92dd288","bundle_entry_key":"2412-03774-theorem-15-coro-4","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_119","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s1_119.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2412-03774-corollary-15-coro-4","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: We can write $g_n(r,v)=e^{\frac{v}{2}}g_{n,1}(r,v)g_{n,2}(r,v)$ where 
\begin{eqnarray}
g_{n,1}(r,v)=\frac{(1+\frac{r-v}{n-1})^{\frac{n-1}{2}}}{(1+\frac{r}{n})^{\frac{n}{2}}}
\end{eqnarray}
and 
\begin{eqnarray}
g_{n,2}(r,v)=\sum_{i=0}^{\frac{n-1}{2}}(2i-1)!!{\frac{n-1}{2}\choose i}\frac{v^i}{(n-1+r-v)^{i}(v+1)^{i+\frac{1}{2}}}.
\end{eqnarray}
Clearly, $g_{n,2}(r,v)$ is decreasing in terms of $r>0$. The function $g_{n,1}(r,v)$ is decreasing in terms of $r$ if and only if $r\ge nv$. As such, $g_n(r,v)$ is also decreasing in terms of $r$ for $r\ge nv$. It follows that if $g_n(nv,v)<1$, then $g_n(r,v)<1$. But, $g_n(nv,v)=h_n(v)$ as defined in~((h_n_v of the paper)). This","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:7d9694ab776191a0464ce9050b62c441973c5f5a3d809943a9d01d9ea318c0f1","revision_digest":"sha256:824b61606785b7d1c54174695d023455bb8c90c2dc08a415572fe3d6165f0e96","source_id":"arxiv:2412.03774","source_revision":"tex-as-downloaded-2026-09-08","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

