[Lemma lem:naive of arXiv:2509.23496, 'Crossing probabilities in geometric inhomogeneous random graphs'] We have, for all $0<u<1$ and $R\ge 4,$
\begin{equation}
    \theta_{(u), R}  \leq \pi_{(u), R/2} + \theta_{R/2, R} .
\end{equation}
Consequently, we have
\begin{equation}
    \theta_{(u), R}  \asymp  \pi_{(u), R}^{(2)}  \text{ on } \widetilde D_0,

\end{equation}
where the domain $\widetilde D_0$ is defined as
\begin{equation}
 \widetilde D_0:=   \begin{cases}
    \{ u^{-1}\ge R^{d } \log R\}& \text{ if }\delta\ge \frac 1 \gamma,\\
    \{ u^{-\gamma \delta}\ge R^d\}& \text{ if }\frac 1 \gamma>\delta.
\end{cases}
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Corollary cor:chim of arXiv:2605.15545, 'Crossover from subcritical to critical decay: \\ random walk, self-avoiding walk, percolat'] Let $d\ge 1$ and $\delta>0$.
Suppose that Assumptions~(ass:Omega of the paper) and~(ass:J of the paper) hold for $S_z$ for each $z\in [z_c-\delta,z_c)$, suppose that $m_z \to 0$ as $z \to z_c$,
and suppose that there are constants $M,K_{\mathrm{IR}},\zeta$ such that
$(J_z^{(\mu)},h_z^{(\mu)}) \in \mathcal{Q}_{M,K_{\mathrm{IR}},\zeta}$ uniformly in $\mu\in \overline\Omega_z$ and in $z \in [z_c-\delta,z_c)$.
Then $\chi(z) \to \infty$ as $z \to z_c$.  More precisely,
\begin{equation}

    \chi(z)
    =
    \frac{2d\hat h_{z}(0)}{\sigma_{z}^2}
      \frac{1}{m_z^{2}}
    \big[ 1+O(m_z^{\zeta\wedge2}) \big] .
\end{equation}

