{"artifact_digest":"sha256:f51454a52b5ba2ca215201320e65239c97fb267384387100366dfb50ce60574d","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/f5/f51454a52b5ba2ca215201320e65239c97fb267384387100366dfb50ce60574d.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:f617b79a380b492a094cc3ee6eb5cbf54e76f335a27927756204526e6b3c8873","bundle_entry_key":"2608-10415-theorem-99-negative-plt","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_052","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s1_052.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2608-10415-theorem-99-negative-plt","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Let us denote by $\nu_m$ the geometric law under the convolution sign:
$$\nu_m=\sum_{k\geq0}\left(\frac{t}{m+t}\right)^k\frac{m}{m+t}\,\delta_k$$

We have the following computation, for the Fourier transform of the limit: 
\begin{eqnarray*}
F_{\delta_k}(x)=e^{ikx}
&\implies&F_{\nu_m}(x)=\sum_{k\geq0}\left(\frac{t}{m+t}\right)^k\frac{m}{m+t}\,e^{ikx}\\
&\implies&F_{\nu_m}(x)=\frac{m}{m+t-te^{ix}}\\
&\implies&F_{\nu_m^{*m}}(x)=\left(\frac{m}{m+t-te^{ix}}\right)^m\\
&\implies&F_{\nu_m^{*m}}(x)=\left(1+\frac{t(e^{ix}-1)}{m+t-te^{ix}}\right)^m\\
&\implies&F(x)=\exp\left(t(e^{ix}-1)\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

Thus, we obtain indeed the Fourier transform of $p_t$, as","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:ff33fd380e463af22663a04e51f8bdb3ffa8e8fa335efb3220bd86dfdb228a89","revision_digest":"sha256:84414d10e8d3cc0c94fec11ea33f081b5c5d584569d05718c75d5f2cca2bb258","source_id":"arxiv:2608.10415","source_revision":"tex-as-downloaded-2026-09-08","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

