[Corollary 12 of arXiv:2606.23099, 'Path-dependent Affine Processes'] In the setting of Theorem~\ref{main_thm}, define the $\ccM([0,T],\C^d)$-valued measure $\Psi$ by
    \begin{align}
        \Psi^j(t,T,\mu,A) & = \psi^j(t)\delta_t(A) + \mu^j(A \cap [0,t)) \notag            \\
                          & \quad + \int_t^T \Bigl( \psi(u) b^j\bigl(u, A \cap [0,t)\bigr)
        + \frac{1}{2} \psi(u)\, a^j\bigl(u, A \cap [0,t)\bigr)\, \psi(u)^\top\Bigr)\, du, \quad j = 1,\dotsc, d,
    \end{align}
    for all Borel sets $A \subseteq [0,T]$.
    Then
    \begin{equation*}
        Y(t)=\phi(t)+\Psi(t,T,\mu)\cdot X_{[0,t]}
    \end{equation*}
    and if $e^Y$ is a true martingale, then the conditional Fourier--Laplace transform admits the exponential-affine representation.
    Moreover, the pair $(\Psi,X)$ satisfies the semimartingale representation of
    Assumption~\ref{ass:sm-diff-Psi} with
    \begin{align*}
        \psi(t,T,\mu)             & = \psi(t)                                                                \\
        \alpha(t,X_{[0,t]},T,\mu) & = \psi(t,T,\mu)B(t,X_{[0,t]}) + \frac{1}{2}A(t,X_{[0,t]},\psi(t,T,\mu)).
    \end{align*}

