{"artifact_digest":"sha256:d161bcba27ed4590c098853c12389a339911a19ea893e48c905bbb9f3e7f2ee6","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/d1/d161bcba27ed4590c098853c12389a339911a19ea893e48c905bbb9f3e7f2ee6.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:5fe61c4a662b4c1757fa2113406412a70097b4367196008a241511a820a6f715","bundle_entry_key":"2603-11043-lemma-37-lem-singular-value-lower-bound","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_101","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_101.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2603-11043-lemma-37-lem-singular-value-lower-bound","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: A basic property of singular value decomposition is that
    \begin{align*}
        & \sigma_1(A) = \|A\| = \max_{\|z\|=1} \| A z \| = \max_{\|z\|=1}\|\sum_i z_i v_i\| 
        \\ & \le \max_i \|v_i\| \max_{\|z\|=1} \|z\|_1 \le \sqrt {n} R.

    \end{align*}
    Here the first inequality follows since $\|\sum_i z_i v_i\| \le \sum_i |z_i| \|v_i\|$ by the triangle inequality and the
    second one follows by ((eq.cauchy_schwarz of the paper)). 

    So using the property $\sigma_1(A) \dots \sigma_n(A) = \sqrt{\det A^T A}$ we have
    \[
        \sigma_{n}(A) \ge \frac { \sqrt{\det A^T A}} {\sigma_1(A)^{n-1}} \ge \frac 1 {\sigma_1(A)^{n-1}} \ge (\sqrt n R)^{-(n-1)}.","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:1036281e0533d45e2c40149d7be2ea63105307d3cc5a8c97b979f58cb2a7cfce","revision_digest":"sha256:8862213a1884b07a48fbeebd6080469b1214f9268b023523c0bae04ad8e7c8f9","source_id":"arxiv:2603.11043","source_revision":"tex-as-downloaded-2026-09-08","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

