[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2608.26038, 'Rotated semicircle laws for permanental roots of Gaussian random matrices'] With the notation above,
\[
  \nu_{P_N}
  \weak
  (\mathsf m_i)_\#\mu_{\mathrm{sc}}
  \qquad\text{almost surely.}
\]
Equivalently, for every bounded continuous function \(f:\C\to\C\),
\[
  \frac1N\sum_{j=1}^N f(\zeta_{j,N})
  \to
  \int_{-2}^{2}
  f(\ii x)\frac1{2\pi}\sqrt{4-x^2}\,\dd x
  \qquad\text{almost surely.}
\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition Valid ordering of arXiv:2603.28936, 'Composition of random functions and word reconstruction'] Given a partition $\mathcal{P}$ (random or not) of $[p]$, an ordering (permutation) $\sigma=(\sigma_1,\dots,\sigma_p)$ of $[p]$ is \emph{valid} for $\mathcal{P}$ if
\begin{itemize}
\item[-] for every $j,j'\in [r]$ with $j<j'$, all the elements of $P_j$ appear before any of the elements of $P_{j'}$ in $\sigma$; and
\item[-] for every $j\in [r]$ and $i\in P_j$, if $i$ is not the first element of $P_j$ to appear in the sequence $\sigma$, then $B_\omega(V_i)$ intersects at least one in-ball $B_\omega(V_{i'})$ for $i'\in P_j$ and $i'$ appearing before $i$ in $\sigma$.
\end{itemize}

[Defn def:betacoal of arXiv:2601.09537, 'Gene genealogies in haploid populations evolving according to sweepstakes reproduction'] The Beta$(\gamma,  2-\alpha,\alpha)$-coalescent is a $\Lambda$-coalescent with
transition rates  as in~(equation eq:19 of the paper)  with $c = 0$,   and   $\Lambda_+$, 
 for  $0 < \alpha  < 2$   and $0 < \gamma  \le 1$,  is 
\begin{equation}

\text{d} \Lambda_+(x) =  \frac{1}{B(\gamma, 2-\alpha,\alpha)} \ensuremath{\mathds{1}_{\left\{ 0 < x \le \gamma  \right\}}}  x^{1-\alpha} {(1-x)}^{\alpha - 1}  \text{d}x
\end{equation}
In~(equation eq:3 of the paper)  $B(p,   a,b) \equiv   \int_{0}^{1}\ensuremath{\mathds{1}_{\left\{ 0 < t \le p  \right\}}}
t^{a-1}{(1-t)}^{b-1}dt$ for   \textcolor{black}{some given } 
$a,b > 0$ and $0 < p \le 1$ all fixed   is the (lower  incomplete when $p < 1$)   beta function.

[Lemma 103 (Raychaudhuri-type transport equation for $\mytr_{\gtorus}\upchi$) of arXiv:2609.03101, 'The emergence of the Cauchy horizon from the crease for $3D$ compressible Euler flow'] The following identity holds:
	\begin{align}  
		\upmu \Lunit \mytr_{\gtorus}\upchi 
		& = \Lunit \upmu \mytr_{\gtorus}\upchi - \upmu \Ricfour_{\Lunit \Lunit} - \upmu |\upchi|^2. 
	\end{align}

[Conjecture conj:main of arXiv:2607.05345, 'The local product condition implies cutoff'] A cutoff occurs as soon as 
		\begin{eqnarray}

			\gamma\times \mathrm{t}_{\textsc{mix}}(\varepsilon) & \xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{} & +\infty,
		\end{eqnarray}
		for some $\varepsilon\in(0,1)$, where $\gamma$ denotes the Poincaré constant of the considered process. (Stated as a conjecture by the paper; open unless the paper says otherwise.)

[Theorem thm:biOPE-varying of arXiv:2411.15830, 'Deformations of biorthogonal ensembles and universality'] Let  $(\mathcal X_n)_{n\in\mathbb N}$ be a sequence of biorthogonal ensembles of the form (equation def:wtP of the paper) with correlation kernels ${\mathsf K}_n$ with a varying reference measure $\mathrm{d}\mu_n$. Let $(\sigma_n)_{n\in\mathbb N}$ be a sequence of Borel measurable functions $\sigma_n:\mathbb K \to[0,1]$. If Assumption (assumption:sigmavarying of the paper) holds, then we have the limit
\begin{equation}
\lim_{n\to\infty}\mathcal G_n^{\sigma_n}[h]=\mathcal G^\sigma[h]
\end{equation}

for any continuous function $h:\mathbb K \to [0,+\infty)$ with bounded support.
In particular, the deformed biorthogonal ensemble $\mathcal X_n^{\sigma_n}$ converges weakly as $n\to\infty$ to the deformed limit point process $\mathcal X^{\sigma}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lem:adm_scaled_exp_delta of arXiv:2607.04514, 'Boundary-layer asymptotics for Gaussian-smoothed singular measures'] Assume (ass:standing of the paper)~(ass:standing:chart of the paper){} with \(r\geqslant 2\). Let \(A,\sigma_0>0\) and \(R_0\in(0,4R)\). Then there
  exists \(\mathscr R_\Delta\in \mathfrak P_{r-2}\) defined on \(\mathcal
  D_{R_0,A,\mathcal{K}_{\mathcal{S}},\sigma_0}\), such that
  \begin{equation}
    \sigma^{-1}\Delta(\theta,\sigma\zeta)
    = \begin{bmatrix} \mathbf{L}(\theta)\\ \mathbf 0_{k\times m}
    \end{bmatrix}\,\zeta + \frac\sigma2\mathrm{D}_\xi^2 \Delta(\theta, 0)[\zeta,\zeta]
    + \sigma^2 \mathscr R_\Delta(\zeta;a,\varphi(\theta),\sigma).
  \end{equation}

