[Proposition prop_1725 of arXiv:2605.10479, 'Poisson approximation of random lattices'] Let $\varphi:\mathcal F(\mathbb R^n)\to [0,\infty)$ be a function such that
$\varphi(Y)=0$ whenever the vectors in $Y$ are linearly dependent.
Let $B\subseteq \mathbb R^n$ be finite, and let $k\geq 0$. Let $R_1,R_2\geq 0$
be integers such that $R_1$ is odd and $R_2$ is even. Then,
\begin{equation*}
\sum_{r=0}^{R_1}
(-1)^r
\sum_{X\in \mathcal S_{k,r}(B)}
\sum_{Y\in {X\choose k}}
\varphi(Y)
\leq
\varphi(B)1_{\{|B|=k\}}
\leq
\sum_{r=0}^{R_2}
(-1)^r
\sum_{X\in \mathcal T_{k,r}(B)}
\sum_{Y\in {X\choose k}}
\varphi(Y).
\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Quest of arXiv:2509.06730, 'Hyperbolic branching Brownian motion: \\ the empirical limit measure'] In Proposition (prop:conv_typical_to_mu of the paper) we have shown that $\mu_\infty$ is determined by particles that satisfy $Y_u(t)\approx e^{-t/2}$. It would be interesting look at the empirical measure of particles that satisfy $Y_u(t)\approx e^{-t/2+\lambda t}$. This should be non--trivial for any $\lambda$ with $\vert \lambda \vert < \sqrt{2\beta}$. In particular, if you look at $\lambda=1/2$ there should {be} a phase transition when $\beta=1/8$ because this is the threshold for local survival. For BBM on $\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}$, the  number of particles {at a given speed} is counted by the additive martingale; note that here in the hyperbolic setting
    \begin{equation*}
        \sum_{u\in \mathcal{N}(t)} (Y_u(t))^\lambda e^{-t(\lambda^2+\lambda-2)/2}
    \end{equation*}
    has the same distribution as the regular additive martingale. (Stated as a question by the paper; open unless the paper says otherwise.)

