{"artifact_digest":"sha256:0cd5025a4811fb8c9e701916df85956f79e446db3fd8ad59fe046086c89ee786","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/0c/0cd5025a4811fb8c9e701916df85956f79e446db3fd8ad59fe046086c89ee786.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:8176106a961dee0c88f34e3e1fd966ba0bbc005648b314dd2e841b33b2f3d1e6","bundle_entry_key":"2607-18194-lemma-96-coupled-white-noises","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_034","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_034.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2607-18194-lemm-96-coupled-white-noises","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: This is clear for $\xi$ since it is just a coordinate projection.
To show that $\eta=\eta_B$ is a white noise, by the $\pi$-$\lambda$ theorem it suffices to compute its finite-dimensional marginals.
For any $n\in\mathbb{N}$ and $f_1,\dots,f_n\in L^2(\mathbb{R}^2)$, we have
\begin{align*}
    (\eta(f_1), \dots,\eta(f_n))
    = (\xi_1(f_1\1_{B^c}),\dots,\xi_1(f_n\1_{B^c}))
    + (\xi_2(f_1\1_{B}),\dots,\xi_2(f_n\1_{B})).
\end{align*}
Since $\xi_1,\xi_2$ are independent white noises, the RHS is the sum of two independent centered multivariate Gaussians, and thus the LHS is a centered multivariate Gaussian.
