[Cor polynomial-log of arXiv:2606.08325, 'Digit Mixing under Polynomial Maps'] Let \(P\in\mathbb R[x]\) have degree \(d\ge2\).  There exists \(A_0=A_0(P)>0\) such that, if for all sufficiently large \(n\),
			\begin{equation}
				v_n\ge A\left(\frac{\log n}{n}\right)^{\frac{1}{d}}
			\end{equation}
			with \(A\ge A_0\), then \(P(X)\) is almost surely absolutely normal. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem thm:characterization of arXiv:2601.10359, 'An Operator It\^o Formula for Volterra Gaussian Processes:\\ The Intrinsic Bracket via Cau'] Let $X$ be a centered Volterra Gaussian process for which the operator It\^o formula (Theorem~(thm:main of the paper)) holds. Suppose $\nu$ is a signed Borel measure on $[0,T]$ such that for all $\varphi\in C^3_b(\mathbb{R})$,
\begin{equation}
\varphi(X_t) = \varphi(0) + \delta(\varphi'(X)\Pi DX\cdot\mathbf{1}_{[0,t]}) + \frac{1}{2}\int_0^t \varphi''(X_s)\,d\nu(s)
\end{equation}
holds in $L^2(\Omega)$ for all $t\in[0,T]$. Then $\nu = \Gamma^X$ (i.e., $d\nu = d\Gamma^X$) on $[0,T]$.

