[Theo Substitute-Feller of arXiv:2411.19543, 'Feller property and convergence for semigroups of time-changed processes'] Assume that $F$ is closed. Then
\begin{enumerate}
\item[\rm(1)] The space $P_F(C_0(X))$ is  $\check{R}_\alpha$-invariant for all $\alpha>0$ and $\lim_{\alpha\to\infty} \|\alpha\check{R}_\alpha u - u\|_\infty = 0$ for all $u\in P_F(C_0(X))$. 
\item[\rm(2)] The space $P_F(C_0(X))$ is $\check{\bf P}_t$-invariant for all $t>0$ and $\lim_{t\to 0}\| \check{\bf P}_t u - u \|_\infty = 0$ for all $u\in P_F(C_0(X))$.
\item[\rm(3)] For all $t\geq 0$ the semigroup $\check{\bf P}_t$ maps continuously $C_0(X)$ into itself. Moreover, it is continuous, i.e., for all $u\in C_0(X)$, the map: $t\in[0,\infty)\to C_0(X),\; t\mapsto \check{\bf P}_t u$ is continuous.
\end{enumerate}

