[Definition def:cS-def of arXiv:2509.08928, 'The near critical random bond Ising model via embedding deformation'] Let $(G,x)$ be a weighted planar graph with the combinatorics of the plane, and let $\mathcal{X}:\Upsilon^\times(G)\to\mathbb{C}$ be a solution to the full system of equations (equation eq:3-terms of the paper) around each quad. We say that $\mathcal{S}=\mathcal{S}_\mathcal{X}:\Lambda(G)\to\mathbb{C}$ is an s-embedding of $(G,x)$ associated with $\mathcal{X}$ if, for each $c\in\Upsilon^\times(G)$, we have\begin{equation}

\mathcal{S}(v^\bullet(c))-\mathcal{S}(v^\circ(c))=(\mathcal{X}(c))^2.
\end{equation}
For $z \in \diamondsuit(G)$, the quadrilateral $\mathcal{S}^\diamond(z) \subset \mathbb{C}$ is the region bounded by the edges connecting the vertices $\mathcal{S}(v_0^\bullet(z))$, $\mathcal{S}(v_0^\circ(z))$, $\mathcal{S}(v_1^\bullet(z))$, and $\mathcal{S}(v_1^\circ(z))$. The s-embedding $\mathcal{S}$ is called \emph{proper} if the quadrilaterals $\mathcal{S}^\diamond(z) = (\mathcal{S}(v_0^\bullet(z)) \mathcal{S}(v_0^\circ(z)) \mathcal{S}(v_1^\bullet(z)) \mathcal{S}(v_1^\circ(z)))$ do not overlap. It is called \emph{non-degenerate} if no quadrilateral $\mathcal{S}^\diamond(z)$ degenerates into a segment. No convexity condition is imposed on the quadrilaterals $\mathcal{S}^\diamond(z)$.

