[Definition d: fourier smoooth of arXiv:2605.22803, 'Persistence of asymptotic variance under transport:\\ from hyperfluctuation to stealthy hy'] Let $p\in[-d, \infty)$ and $f\in L^1(\R^d, \C)$. Suppose that there is a $c>0$ such that
    \begin{equation}
        |\hat{f}(k)| \leq c \frac{1}{(1+\|k\|)^{\frac{d+p}{2}}}, \quad k\in\R^d.
    \end{equation}
    If $p\leq 0$, then also suppose that, as $r\to\infty$,
    \begin{equation}
        \int_{B_2^c} \sup_{s\in B_1} |\hat{f}(r(k+s))|^2\, dk = O(r^{-(d+p)}).
    \end{equation}
    Then $f$ is called \textit{Fourier-smooth with exponent} $p$.

[Theorem 11 of arXiv:2506.20411, 'Maximal Counts in the Stopped Occupancy Problem'] For every $K\in{\mathbb Z}_{>0}$, as $n\to\infty$,
