body:
[Definition def:malliavin-cov of arXiv:2608.21805, 'Logarithmic derivatives of variational and singular stochastic partial differential equati'] For $X(t) \in \mathbb{D}^{1,2}(H)$, the Malliavin derivative $\mathcal{D}_r X(t)$ at time $r \in [0, t]$ admits the explicit representation in terms of the first variation process and the diffusion coefficient:
\begin{equation}
  \Phi_r \;:=\; \mathcal{D}_r X(t) \;=\; Y(t,r)\,\mathcal{B}(r, X(r)) \;\in\; L_2(U, H)
\end{equation}
(this representation is proved in Proposition~(prop:D-Xt of the paper) of Section~(sec:malliavin of the paper)); the identification $\Phi_r = Y(t,r)\mathcal{B}(r, X(r))$ is a Hilbert--Schmidt-valued equation in $L_2(U, H)$, not a pathwise operator product on $H$ (since $Y(t,r) \in L(H)$ a.s.\ is not part of the abstract framework). The \emph{Malliavin covariance operator} is the trace-class self-adjoint positive operator
\begin{equation}
  \gamma_t \;:=\; \int_0^t \Phi_r\,\Phi_r^{*}\,\mathrm{d}r \;:\; H \to H,
\end{equation}
where $\Phi_r^{*} \in L_2(H, U)$ is the Hilbert--Schmidt adjoint of $\Phi_r$. The intrinsic definition used throughout is
\[
  \gamma_t \;=\; \int_0^t \Phi_r\Phi_r^{*}\,\mathrm{d}r .
\]
Whenever additional equation-specific structure provides an almost surely bounded realisation $Y(t,r) \in L(H)$, the familiar factored expression involving $Y(t,r)^{*}$ may also be used. No pathwise adjoint $Y(t,r)^{*}: H \to H$ is required in the abstract framework.

[Thm 1 ($\sigma$-Martingale Criterion) of arXiv:2606.20925, 'Explicit Predictable Compensators for Single Jump Processes with Initial Information'] Let $X$ be an adapted right-continuous process of finite variation with the canonical representation $X_t=F(t)\ind_{\{t<\gamma\}}+L\ind_{\{t\ge\gamma\}}$. The process $X$ is a $\sigma$-martingale in the filtration $\FF$ if and only if there exists a strictly positive predictable process $\varphi$, defined by an $\cH$-measurable function $\varphi(t)$, such that the following two conditions hold:
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
        \item Local integrability of the localized jump:
        \begin{equation}
            \EE\left[\varphi(\gamma)|L-F(\gamma-)|\right]<\infty.
        \end{equation}
        \item The drift balance conditions: on the interval $s<t_G$, the differential equation holds,
        \begin{equation}
            \overline{G}_{\cH}(s-)dF(s)+F(s)dG_{\cH}(s)+H(s)dG_{\cH}(s)=0
        \end{equation}
        and at the terminal atom $t_G$, the boundary condition holds,
        \begin{equation}
            H(t_G)=F(t_G-).
        \end{equation}
    \end{enumerate}

