[Lemma lem:joint-filtration of arXiv:2608.15515, '\LARGE Weak-Type Bounds for Convolution on the Boolean Hypercube'] For every bounded terminal test $h:G\to\mathbb R$ and every
$\theta\leq t\leqT_{\mathrm o}$,
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb E[h(V_{T_{\mathrm o}})\mid\mathcal F_t]
 =P^V_{t,T_{\mathrm o}}h(V_t).
\end{equation}
For the reverse process already defined on $[0,T]$, the increments of the
driving Poisson random measures after $T_{\mathrm o}$ are independent of
$\mathcal F_{T_{\mathrm o}}$.  Consequently, for every bounded $\Phi:G\to\mathbb R$,
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb E[\Phi(V_T)\mid\mathcal F_t]
 =P^V_{t,T}\Phi(V_t),
 \qquad \theta\leq t\leqT_{\mathrm o}.
\end{equation}
In particular, if
$H_t^\zeta(x)=P^V_{t,T}\1_{\{\zeta\}}(x)$, then
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb P(V_T=\zeta\mid\mathcal F_t)=H_t^\zeta(V_t).
\end{equation}

[Prop 16 of arXiv:2608.30415, 'On generalized complex projective product spaces'] Let $n_1\leq \dots \leq n_{\ell}$ and  $N$ be a smooth manifold having free circle action denoted by $\tau$. Suppose $p_j\geq 1$ for $1\leq j\leq \ell$. Then
\begin{equation}
  \cl(N/\left<\tau\right >)+\ell+1\leq \ct(C((\overline{n}, \overline{p})(\ell),N))\leq \ct(N/\left<\tau\right >)+\ell .  
\end{equation}

[Definition Def-signedmeasure-ring of arXiv:2411.12623, 'Random signed measures'] A set function $\mu(A)$ defined on the elements of a ring $\mathcal{R}$ with values in $[-\infty,\infty]$ will be called countably additive, if $\mu(\emptyset)=0$ and if for every sequence $A_{1},A_{2}, . . .$ of disjoint sets of $\mathcal{R}$ for which $A=\bigcup_{k=1}^{\infty}A_{k}\in\mathcal{R}$ we have
	\begin{equation}
		\mu(A)=\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\mu(A_{k}),
	\end{equation}
	and the relation ((measure on a ring of the paper)) holds absolutely (namely independent of the order of its elements).

[Definition def:nested of arXiv:2609.02234, 'An $n(\log n)^{o(1)}$ bound for nested cycles without geometric crossings'] A sequence $C_1,\ldots,C_k$ of cycles in a graph is a family of \emph{$k$ nested cycles without geometric crossings} if
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $V(C_k)\subseteq V(C_{k-1})\subseteq\cdots\subseteq V(C_1)$;
  \item the cycles are pairwise edge-disjoint; and
  \item for every $i<k$, the cyclic order induced by $C_i$ on $V(C_{i+1})$ agrees, up to reversal, with the cyclic order of $C_{i+1}$.
\end{enumerate}
For $k\ge2$, let $f_k(n)$ be the least integer such that every $n$-vertex graph with at least $f_k(n)$ edges contains such a family.

[Theorem 101 (Slightly supercritical) of arXiv:2507.07702, 'Observables of random spanning trees in random environment'] Let $p= \frac{1 + \epsilon}{n}$ with $\epsilon^3 n \rightarrow \infty$. Then for any $\delta > 0$
\begin{equation*}
        \P \Bigg( \Big| \frac{|\cC_1(p)|}{2 \epsilon n } -1 \Big| \geq \delta \Bigg) \rightarrow 0,
    \end{equation*}
    and $\cC_\ell$, for any $\ell \geq 2$, satisfies \eqref{eq:s-sub-critical}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

