{"artifact_digest":"sha256:2dc7aac1b45f42c84dbba6932873eb926336cb2a221665a0e1b6dfdda2919464","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/2d/2dc7aac1b45f42c84dbba6932873eb926336cb2a221665a0e1b6dfdda2919464.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:08649ca9deb4b13d2782beee35a150d57a40837f7b7ae18c900229e7ebb42540","bundle_entry_key":"2606-28575-lemma-12-nonlinear-est","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_037","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s1_037.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2606-28575-lemma-12-nonlinear-est","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: The second statement easily follows from the first one thanks to the bilinearity of the operator $\mathcal{L}$. Indeed, it holds
    \begin{equation*}
        \mathcal{L}_u\omega - \mathcal{L}_{v}\xi =  \nabla \times (\operatorname{div}(u\otimes u- v\otimes v)) = \nabla \times \left(\operatorname{div} \left[(u-v)\otimes u + v \otimes (u-v)\right]\right).
    \end{equation*}
    We prove the first statement. We have 
    \begin{align*}
