body:
[Theorem thrm:suplinpafit of arXiv:2411.18749, 'A star is born: Explosive Crump--Mode--Jagers branching processes'] Suppose that $f(i,w)=g(w)s(i)$, where $s$ satisfies Assumption~(ass:s of the paper) and $g$ and $s$ satisfy~(equation eq:gsass of the paper). Suppose there exists a sequence $(k_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ and constants $\varepsilon>0, \delta\in(0,\varepsilon)$, and $n_0\in\mathbb{N}$,  such that both 
	\begin{equation} 
	\mathbb{P}\!\left(g(W)>k_n\right)\leq n^{-(1+\varepsilon)}, \qquad\text{for all }n\geq n_0,
	\end{equation} 
	and
	\begin{equation} 
	\lim_{n\to\infty} \frac{\mu_{(\delta\log(n)/(\mu_n k_n))^{1/\beta}}}{\mu_nk_n}=\infty
	\end{equation}  
	are satisfied, where $\beta$ is as in Assumption~(ass:s of the paper). Then, the limiting infinite tree $T_\infty$ contains a unique infinite star and no infinite path, almost surely. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

