[Proposition 10 of arXiv:2607.10725, 'From the Airy line ensemble to the Gaussian free field'] Fix $m \geq 1$, $A \geq 1$, and let $h_T$ be as in (\ref{Eq.HeightFunction}). There exists a constant $C_2(m,A) > 0$ such that for all $T \geq e$ and $t \in \mathbb{R}$
\begin{equation}
\E[h_T(t, -A)^m] \leq C_2(m,A) \cdot T^{3m/2}.
\end{equation}
In addition, if $\e \in (0,1)$ there exists a constant $C_2'(m,\e) > 0$ such that for all $T \geq e$ and $t \in \mathbb{R}$
\begin{equation}
\E[h_T(t, -T^{-\e})^m] \leq C_2'(m,\e) \cdot T^{3m(1-\e)/2}.
\end{equation}

