body:
[Definition 5 of arXiv:2608.04853, 'Minimal-Norm Extensions of Stationary Kernels on Subgroups of Locally Compact Abelian Grou'] We call a function $k:G \times G \to \R$ a kernel if there exists a Hilbert space $H_0$ and a feature map $\Phi_0:G \to H_0$ such that 
\begin{align*}
    k(x,y)=\langle \Phi_0(x),\Phi_0(y)\rangle_{H_0}, \qquad  \forall x,y \in G.
\end{align*}
We call a kernel $k$ stationary if there exists a function $\hat{k} \in L^1(\hat{G}) \cap L^\infty(\hat{G})$ with $\hat{k}: \hat{G} \to \R $ such that $\hat{k} \geq 0$ almost everywhere and 
\begin{align*}
    k(x,y)= \int_{\hat{G}} \hat{k}(\xi) \xi(x  -y) \d \xi, \qquad \forall x,y \in G.
\end{align*}

