[Theorem 5 (Four-flattening second-chaos inequality) of arXiv:2606.26065, 'Localized Centered Second-Chaos Operators'] Let $2\le p\le r<\infty$, and suppose $F_1,F_2,F_3,F_4\in\Sch_r$.  Let $Z_{\mathbf H}:\cC\to\cE$ be the decoupled second-chaos operator associated with $\mathbf H$.  Then
\begin{equation}
 \left\|\,\norm{Z_{\mathbf H}}_{\cC\to\cE}\,\right\|_{L^p(\Omega)}
 \le C r\sum_{j=1}^4\norm{F_j}_{\Sch_r},
\end{equation}
where $C$ is universal; the displayed linear factor $r$ is the only dependence on the chosen Schatten exponent in this estimate.  The map $\mathbf H\mapsto Z_{\mathbf H}$ extends continuously from finite-rank forms to all forms with $F_j\in\Sch_r$.  The same conclusion holds for the completely Wick-centered same-family chaos, up to the universal order-two decoupling constant.

[Proposition eq:l2esti of arXiv:2505.12187, 'Speeding up quantum Markov processes through lifting'] Let $\exp(tQ)$ be a symmetric ergodic Markov semigroup on $\{1,\ldots,n\}$, and let $\mathcal{L}_\gamma = \mathcal{L}_H + \gamma \mathcal{L}_S$ ($\gamma > 0$) be the Lindbladian defined through (equation eq:singlejump of the paper) and (equation eq:hamq of the paper), where $A$ has $n$ distinct eigenvalues $\{\kappa_i\}_{i=1}^n$ with $\kappa_i = \Theta(1)$.
    Then, it holds that
    \begin{itemize}
        \item  $\mathcal{L}_\gamma$ is primitive and hypocoercive with the maximally mixed state $\frac{{\bf 1}}{n}$ as the unique invariant state. Moreover, $\exp(t \mathcal{L}_\gamma)$ is a lift of $\exp(t Q)$ in the sense of (def:generallift of the paper).
        \item $\mathcal{L}_\gamma$ has the maximal 
     $L^2$ convergence rate estimate at a particular $\gamma_{\max}$:
    \begin{align} 
        \Omega\left( \frac{\sqrt{\lambda_Q}}{1  +   K_1  + K_2 \lambda_Q^{-1/2}} \right) \leq \nu(\mathcal{L}_\gamma) \leq \mathcal{O}(\sqrt{\lambda_Q})\,,
    \end{align}
    where $\lambda_Q$ denotes the spectral gap of $Q$, and the constants $K_1, K_2 > 0$ satisfy  
    \begin{align} 
          \lVert[H,[H, Y]]\rVert_{2,\frac{{\bf 1}}{n}} \le K_1 \lVertQ y\rVert_{\ell^2(\nu)} + K_2 \sqrt{- \langle y, Q y\rangle_{\ell^2(\nu)}}\,,
    \end{align}
    for $Y = \sum_j y_j \lvertj\rangle\langlej\rvert \in \ker(\mathcal{L}_S)$. 
    \end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Corollary of arXiv:2603.28936, 'Composition of random functions and word reconstruction'] Let $\pi$ be a primitive word.
Let $L\geq 1$ and let $r_1,\dots,r_L\geq 0$.
then
\begin{align}
	\mathbb E \left[ (C^\pi_{1,n})_{r_1} \cdots (C^\pi_{L,n})_{r_L}\right] = \frac{1}{1^{r_1} 2^{r_2}\cdots L^{r_L}} + O\left(\frac{1}{n}\right).
\end{align}

