[Theorem T:Haus:meas of arXiv:2604.08129, 'Polarity of points for Gaussian random fields in critical dimension'] Let Assumptions (a:X of the paper) and (a:SLND of the paper) hold with $\sigma$ given by (equation sigma:gamma of the paper), where $d=N/H$ and $\gamma \le 1/d$. Let $I$ be a compact interval in ${\mathbb R}^N$. Then a.s., the range $X(I)$ has finite Hausdorff measure with respect to the gauge function $\phi(r)$ defined by
\begin{align}
    \phi(r) =  r^d (\log(1/r))^{1-\gamma d}\log\log\log(1/r).
\end{align}
In particular, $X(I)$ has Lebesgue measure zero a.s. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

