{"artifact_digest":"sha256:3661b5b210483538cc696029784b864d316da8c8810378549e2136bcdea448f7","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/36/3661b5b210483538cc696029784b864d316da8c8810378549e2136bcdea448f7.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:18cd5ad9670bc2d5d1e10a4d92817c33cec0e6b984d6848741e6a937a8e10e80","bundle_entry_key":"2608-17449-lemma-22-finite-dimensional-splitting","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_098","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_098.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2608-17449-lemma-22-finite-dimensional-splitting","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Choose a finite real linear combination of the operators
$C_q^\Lambda(\mu)$ having the largest possible number of distinct eigenvalues,
and denote it by $C_{\max}$.  If $C_{\max}$ had fewer than $r$ distinct eigenvalues,
some eigenvalue $\alpha$ would have an eigenspace
\begin{equation*}
 E_\alpha:=\ker(C_{\max}-\alpha I),
 \qquad s:=\dim E_\alpha\ge2.
\end{equation*}
Choose orthonormal $u,v\in E_\alpha$.  By
\cref{lem:non-scalar-compression}, there is $q\in Q$ for which
\begin{equation*}
 \left(
  \langle C_q^\Lambda(\mu)u,v\rangle_{L^2(\mu)},
  \langle C_q^\Lambda(\mu)u,u\rangle_{L^2(\mu)}
  -\langle C_q^\Lambda(\mu)v,v\rangle_{L^2(\mu)}
