body:
[Definition DEF33 of arXiv:2412.08886, 'On domination for (non-symmetric) Dirichlet forms'] A real-valued process $M=(M_t)_{t\geq 0}$ is called a \emph{multiplicative functional} (MF, for short) of $X$ if it satisfies the following conditions:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(1)] The map $t\mapsto M_t(\omega)$ is decreasing,  right continuous and takes values in $[0,1]$ for all $\omega\in \Omega$;
\item[(2)] $M$ is adapted, i.e., $M_t\in \mathcal{F}_t$ for every $t\geq 0$;
\item[(3)] $M_{t+s}(\omega)=M_t(\omega)M_s(\theta_t\omega)$ holds for all $s,t\geq 0$ and $\omega\in \Omega$.
\end{itemize}

[Lem l:8.2 of arXiv:2602.21277, 'A Limit in Law for the Cover Time and Last Visited Vertex of Wired Planar Domains'] For all $n$ large enough, $x \in {\rm D}_n^\circ$ and $y \in \partial {\rm B}(x; r_n)$, 
	\begin{equation}
		\mathbb{P}_y \big(\tau_{\partial} < \widecheck{{\mathbf T}}_{{\rm B}(x;r_n)} \big) \leq n^{-\eta_0/3}\,,
	\end{equation}
	where $\eta_0$ is as in~(equation e:2.3 of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition 18 of arXiv:2604.01978, 'Homogenized Transformers'] Let $X^n = §(x^{n}_1,\ldots, x^{n}_1)$ solve 
\begin{equation*}
\de x^{n}_i(t)=\int_{\Uptheta}G_{\mu^{n}(t)}(x_{i}^n(t);\bm{\theta})\mathsf{W}(\de \bm{\theta}, \de t),
\end{equation*}
with $\mu^n = \mu_{X^n}$. 
Let $(\bar x_1,\ldots,\bar x_n)$ be conditionally i.i.d., given $(\mathscr{F}^{\mathsf W}_t)_{t\geq0}$, copies of the unique solution $x$ of \eqref{eq:non_linear_SDE_common}, driven by the same common noise $\mathsf{W}$, and let $\bar\mu$ be their empirical measure. Then for every $T>0$ there exists $C_T>0$ such that 
\begin{align*}
    \max_{i\in[n]} \E \sup_{t\in[0, T]} \left\|x^{n}_i(t)-\bar x_i(t)\right\|^2 &+ \E \sup_{t\in[0, T]} W_2^2(\mu^n(t), \bar\mu^n(t)) \\
    &\leq  \frac{C_T}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n \E \left\|x^{n,i}(0)-\bar x^i(0)\right\|^2.
\end{align*}
In particular, after choosing an optimal coupling of the initial data, the right hand side may be written as $C_T \E W_2^2(\mu^n(0), \bar \mu^n(0))$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 8 of arXiv:2506.17045, '\LARGE{On bivariate Archimax copulas: Level sets, mass distributions and related results}'] Let $\psi \in \mathbf{\Psi}$, $A \in \mathcal{A}$, $C_{\psi,A} \in \mathcal{C}_{am}$ and let $L$, $R$ be defined according to eq. \eqref{eq:definition_L_R}. Then 
    \begin{equation}
    \mathrm{supp}(\mu_{C_{\psi,A}}) \subseteq S_{L,R}
    \end{equation}
    holds.

[Prop pr:stopping_time_lower_bound of arXiv:2411.03482, 'Ergodicity of 2D singular stochastic Navier--Stokes equations'] Suppose the initial data has a decomposition $u = u_{\mathfrak{r}} + u_{\mathfrak{s}}$ with $\|u_{\mathfrak{r}}\|_{\mathcal{C}^{-\kappa}} \leq 2\alpha_0$ and $\|u_{\mathfrak{s}}\| = \lambda$\,, then for any $a > 0$ sufficiently small, we have 
    \begin{equ}

        \mathbb{P}(T^{-1} > a^{-1})\lesssim a^{\frac{1}{8}-\kappa}\;,
    \end{equ}
    where the proportional constant is independent of $\lambda$, $\alpha_0$ and $a$\,.

