Let X be a topological space and let (f_i) be an arbitrary family of functions on X with values in the extended reals. If every f_i is upper semicontinuous - in particular if every f_i is continuous - then the pointwise infimum inf_i f_i is upper semicontinuous. Dually, a pointwise supremum of lower semicontinuous functions is lower semicontinuous. The family may be uncountable, and no uniformity is needed. The DIRECTION matters and is the standard place to slip: a supremum of upper semicontinuous functions need not be upper semicontinuous, and an infimum of lower semicontinuous functions need not be lower semicontinuous.
No finiteness, countability or uniformity assumption on the family;
values in the extended reals are allowed.

[Lemma eq:oxCovVV of arXiv:2511.06321, 'Torus scaling limits and the plateau of the critical weakly coupled $|\varphi|^4$ model in'] For $V_j \in \cV$ and $j < N$,
\begin{align}
	\sigma_{\sf ox}^{-1} \operatorname{Cov}_{\pi,j+1} [ \theta V_y  ; \theta V_z ] &= \sum_{\km_3, \km_4 \in \ko_1 \cup \ko_{1,\nabla}} \Big( \lambda^{(\km_3)}_{{\sf o}} \lambda^{(\km_4)}_{{\sf x}} \nabla^{\km_3}_{y}  \nabla^{\km_4}_{z} \Gamma_{j+1} (y-z) \one_{y={\sf o}, z={\sf x}} 
	 \\
		& \qquad\qquad\qquad + \lambda^{(\km_3)}_{{\sf x}} \lambda^{(\km_4)}_{{\sf o}} \nabla^{\km_4}_{x'}  \nabla^{\km_3}_{x''} \Gamma_{j+1} (y-z) \one_{y={\sf x}, z={\sf o}} \Big)  \nonumber
\end{align}
and
\begin{align}
	\sigma_{{\sf ox}}^{-1} W_{j} &= 0 .

\end{align}

